ok, now conceptually, we also need to model interventions. Our main intervention is early detection or prediction of the risk of adverse therapy side effects in terms of cancer therapy toxicity. what follows from such intervention must be modeled as well based on additional model parameters. the risk model with its parametrization will be added later to the app, once the basic app has been stabilized. 

The effects can include early treatment at lower cost and improved outcome as compared to late detection of the toxicitiy that creates more harm and costs. Think about this for a moment and come up with realistic, reasonable, and useful intervention-effect model components. 

we can then integrate the intervention model into our microsimulation - and create multiple "parallel universes" of how the cohort could have evolved with different interventions in place. these could then be explored in the app later in a cohort and intervention analytics mode.

of course, also include that interventions come with participation rates, dropout (patients that do not show up anymore after diagnosis) etc.

